Find the union of each of the following pairs of sets :
(i) A= {x: x is a natural number and 1< x ? 6}
B= {x: x is a natural number and 6< x ? 10}
(ii) A= {1, 2, 3}, B= ?

Answer:
R= {(x, y): The difference between x and y is odd; x ? A, y ? B}
where, A= {1, 2, 3, 5} and B= {4, 6, 9}

R= {(1, 4), (1, 6), (2, 9), (3, 4), (3, 6), (5, 4), (5, 6)}

## Image

The second elements of each ordered pair of the relation R is called the image of the first element.

## Domain of a Relation

Domain of a relation is the set of all first elements of the ordered pairs in the relation.
So, domain of the relation {(2, 3), (2, 4), (2, 5), (3, 4), (3, 5)} = {2, 3}

## Range of a Relation

Range of a relation is the set of all second elements of the ordered pairs in the relation.
So, range of the relation {(2, 3), (2, 4), (2, 5), (3, 4), (3, 5)} = {3, 4, 5}

## Co-domain of a Relation

The set B is known as the codomain of a relation from a non-empty set A to non-empty set B.
Consider the relation R from set A to B, where A= {2, 3, 8} and B= {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
Then, codomain of the relation R is B= {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

Note: 1. Rang ? Co-domain.
2. If there is a relation from a non-empty set A to the same non-empty set A, then it said that 'relation in set A '.

For example, a relation 'less than' in set A= {2, 3, 8} is {(2, 3), (2, 8), (3, 8)}

## FUNCTION

A relation ' f ' from a non-empty set A to a non-empty set B is symbolically written as f: A ? B, is said to be function if every element of set A has a unique image in set
B. Since function is a special type of relation. So, it has also domain, codomain and range as of relation.
Like a relation, for the function f: A ? B, set A is called the domain and set B is called the codomain of the function f.

For example : Let us consider a relation from set A to B, where
A= {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, B= {? 5, ? 4, ? 3, ? 2, 0, 1, 2, 5} and the
relation is defined by the equation y= x² ? 4 x.
Hence, the relation = {(0, 0), (1, ? 3), (2, ? 4), (3, ? 3), (4, 0), (5, 5)}
This relation can be also shown diagramatically in the figure.
Here, we see that each element of set A is related with unique (only one) element of set B. Hence, the above relation is a function.
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/9afb5168-3a1c-43f6-9248-79825029b395-508.jpg?height=476&width=332&top_left_y=372&top_left_x=1395)

Domain of the function = A= {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
Range of the function = {? 4, ? 3, 0, 5}
Co-domain of the function = B= {? 5, ? 4, ? 3, ? 2, ? 1, 0, 1, 2, 5}
Example : Let consider another relation from set C to D, where C= {4, 9, 16, 25}, D= {? 3, ? 1, 0, 2, 3, 4, 5} and the relation defined by the equation y= ?(x).
Hence, the relation = {(4, 2), (9, ? 3), (9, 3), (16, 4), (25, 5)}
This relation can also be shown in the figure.
Here, we see that an element 9 of set C is related with two elements -3 and 3 of set D.
Hence, each element of set C is not related with the unique (only one) element of set D.
Therefore, the given relation is not a function.

Question:
Which of the following relations are functions? Give reasons. If it is a function, determine its domain and range.
(i) {(2, 1), (5, 1), (8, 1), (11, 1), (14, 1), (17, 1)}
(ii) {(2, 1), (4, 2), (6, 3), (8, 4), (10, 5), (12, 6), (14, 7)}
(iii) {(1, 3), (1, 5), (2, 5)}

Answer:
(i) We have, f= {(2, 1), (5, 1), (8, 1), (11, 1), (14, 1), (17, 1)}

No two ordered pairs have the same first component, i.e. each element of the domain is uniquely related to the element of codomain, therefore this relation is a function.
Domain (f) = {2, 5, 8, 11, 14, 17}, Range (f) = {1}
(ii) We have, f= {(2, 1), (4, 2), (6, 3), (8, 4), (10, 5), (12, 6), (14, 7)}

We observe that no two ordered pairs have the same first component, so f is a function.
Domain (f) = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14}; Range (f) = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7}
(iii) f= {(1, 3), (1, 5), (2, 5)}

We observe that 1 has appeared more than once as first component of the ordered pairs in f i.e. each element of the domain is not uniquely related to the element of codomain,
Therefore, f is not a function.

