Question:
Which of the following relations are functions? Give reasons. If it is a function, determine its domain and range.
(i) {(2, 1), (5, 1), (8, 1), (11, 1), (14, 1), (17, 1)}
(ii) {(2, 1), (4, 2), (6, 3), (8, 4), (10, 5), (12, 6), (14, 7)}
(iii) {(1, 3), (1, 5), (2, 5)}

Answer:
(i) We have, f= {(2, 1), (5, 1), (8, 1), (11, 1), (14, 1), (17, 1)}

No two ordered pairs have the same first component, i.e. each element of the domain is uniquely related to the element of codomain, therefore this relation is a function.
Domain (f) = {2, 5, 8, 11, 14, 17}, Range (f) = {1}
(ii) We have, f= {(2, 1), (4, 2), (6, 3), (8, 4), (10, 5), (12, 6), (14, 7)}

We observe that no two ordered pairs have the same first component, so f is a function.
Domain (f) = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14}; Range (f) = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7}
(iii) f= {(1, 3), (1, 5), (2, 5)}

We observe that 1 has appeared more than once as first component of the ordered pairs in f i.e. each element of the domain is not uniquely related to the element of codomain,
Therefore, f is not a function.

